@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
title Workspace scan — one walker, monorepo-aware, dependency trees pruned

node "internal/wsscan" as PKG <<host>> {
  component "Scan(scopeDir, repoRoot,\nmarkers, budget) Result" as SCAN <<app>>
  component "alwaysPrune\nhardcoded deny set" as HARD <<app>>
  component "gitignoreNames(dir)\nplain basenames only" as GI <<app>>
}

folder "Consumers" as USE <<host>> {
  component "choice prompt header\ntooling · lsp prediction" as HDR <<app>>
}

database "workspace tree\ndepth <= MaxDepth (7)" as TREE <<db>>
component "unpruned dependency tree\n→ a dependency's files read as the project's" as BAD <<error>>

SCAN ARROW_MAIN HARD : always applied
SCAN ARROW_OPTIONAL GI : scope AND repo root, additive
SCAN ARROW_MAIN TREE : walk, budgeted
HDR ARROW_MAIN SCAN
GI ARROW_ERROR BAD : gitignore alone is not enough

note right of GI
  **Why .gitignore alone was unsafe — the bug this replaced.**

  Lookup stopped at the FIRST ""\.gitignore"" found walking up, so a
  monorepo scope with its own file never saw the repo root's
  ""node_modules"" entry: ""proveo run --scope apps/web"" with an
  ""apps/web/\.gitignore"" listing only ""\.next/"" walked straight into
  ""apps/web/node_modules"" to depth 7.

  Two more holes: any pattern containing / * ? [ is unusable as a
  basename prune (""**/node_modules"" never matched), and a repo
  relying on ""core.excludesFile"" supplied nothing at all.

  So the derived names are now **additive only** — read from the
  scope //and// the repo root — on top of a set that always applies.
end note

note bottom of BAD
  **Not merely wasteful — wrong, and it once decided a posture.**
  ""dind.ScopeHasDockerfiles"" fed the privileged sidecar's offer, and npm
  packages ship Dockerfiles: a repo with none of its own could be offered a
  ""--privileged"" sibling because of a dependency. That gate is gone with the
  sidecar (_spec/_plans/retire-dind.puml) and the walker's remaining caller
  only labels a prompt — but the deny set stays hardcoded rather than
  discovered, because a scan that reports a dependency's files as the
  project's is wrong whatever reads it.
end note

note bottom of TREE
  **Bounded three ways.** Depth stops at ""MaxDepth"" (7, matching the
  walker this replaced). ""DefaultBudget"" caps entries examined — the
  scan runs before the container exists, in front of an operator at a
  prompt, so a pathological tree must degrade rather than stall. And
  the walk exits as soon as every marker is found, which in practice is
  shallow: files at each level are read before descending.

  A bounded scan sets ""Result.Truncated"". That is **logged, never
  shown** in the prompt header: the header stays about the workspace,
  and an operator who needs to know finds it in the run log.
end note

note left of HARD
  **One deny set, three walks.** ""AlwaysPrune"" is exported because the
  same question is asked by this scan, by mount planning's symlink walk
  (""_spec/internal/workspace/mount-symlink-escape\.puml"") and by the
  in-container LSP detector's ""find"" expression — and three private
  copies drift the moment one is corrected.

  Package stores earn their place on it. ""\.pnpm-store"" was 110k of the
  123k files the LSP detector crossed on one monorepo, and a store
  object can never be workspace source: it is content-addressed and has
  no extension. This scan's budget usually stops before reaching one,
  which is why the names arrived late.
end note

note right of SCAN
  **Markers, not globs.** ""Names"" match a basename exactly, ""Suffixes""
  match its end (""\.go""), and either satisfies the marker. Tooling
  labels are reported as fact; LSP labels are a **prediction** — whether
  a server exists depends on the image, which the host cannot see.
end note
@enduml
